        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.74,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadrilaterals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Carpenter’s Problem shows that rectangles can also be defined as follows—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.13,
            0.848,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rectangle:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A rectangle is a quadrilateral whose diagonals are equal and bisect each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.171,
            0.848,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe how different this definition is from the earlier one. Yet, both capture the same class of quadrilaterals. Further, it turns out that the first definition can be simplified."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.238,
            0.848,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ In the earlier definition, we stated that a rectangle has (a) opposite sides of equal length, and (b) all angles equal to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "9 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Would we be wrong if we just define a rectangle as a quadrilateral in which all the angles are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "9 0 ^ { \\circ } ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.306,
            0.848,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ If you think that this definition is incomplete, try constructing a quadrilateral in which the angles are all "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "9 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " but the opposite sides are not equal."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.367,
            0.611,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are you able to construct such a quadrilateral? Let us prove why this is impossible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.412,
            0.847,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Deduction 4—What is the shape of a quadrilateral with all the",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " angles equal to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { 9 0 ^ { \\circ } ? }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.459,
            0.847,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Consider a quadrilateral ABCD with all angles measuring "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "9 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . What can we say about the opposite sides of such a quadrilateral?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.503,
            0.811,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Join BD. ∆BAD and ∆DCB seem congruent. Can we justify this claim?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.531,
            0.483,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.153,
                0.531,
                0.483,
                0.688
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.489,
            0.579,
            0.848,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Two equalities can be directly seen in the triangles. What can we say about "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and ∠2?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.695,
            0.846,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Recall that we tackled a very similar problem in Deduction 2. We can use the same reasoning here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.735,
            0.483,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": [
